%[text] # Week 12 Lecture Demonstration: Choosing and Auditing a Numerical Method
%[text] **LECTURE DEMONSTRATION**
%[text] PHY4605 Physics-First Reproducible MATLAB Notebook
%%
%[text] ## Physical Question and Learning Outcomes
%[text] A computational result is useful only when the method matches the physical question and the implementation preserves the model. We will recognise familiar method patterns, audit a supplied Euler update, compare two timesteps with an exact cooling reference, and identify the evidence a capstone result must carry.
%[text] By the end of this demonstration, you should be able to match a familiar question to a method, its inputs and limitation; trace, audit and validate a supplied computation; and identify the evidence needed to reproduce and defend a capstone result.
%%
%[text] ## Recognise the Method Before Coding
%[text] Start with the physical question. A rate that changes a state suggests an ODE update; a total accumulated from sampled values suggests numerical integration; a repeated trial suggests Monte Carlo; a relation fitted to observations suggests data fitting; and a formula evaluated for several controlled inputs suggests a parameter sweep. These are learned patterns, not new methods for Week 12.
method_context = ["Newton cooling";"Projectile range";"Work from sampled force";"Linear calibration data";"Random estimate of pi"]; 
method_signal = ["rate changes a temperature";"range is evaluated for several angles";"force is accumulated over position";"parameters are inferred from measurements";"many random trials estimate an area"]; 
method_name = ["Forward Euler ODE";"Parameter sweep";"Trapezoidal integration";"Data fitting";"Monte Carlo sampling"]; 
method_input = ["current temperature, time, tau";"speed, angle, gravity";"force samples and position";"measurements and model variables";"random points and trial count"]; 
method_output = ["temperature versus time";"range versus angle";"work in joules";"fit parameters and residuals";"estimated pi and spread"]; 
method_limitation = ["finite timestep and cooling assumptions";"level landing and no drag";"sample spacing and force model";"noise and model choice";"random variability and sample size"]; 
method_recognition_table = table(method_context,method_signal,method_name,method_input,method_output,method_limitation) %[output:5617d530]
%[text] The method name follows from the input-output relationship. A method label alone is incomplete: the limitation tells us what evidence to check.
%%
%[text] ## Predict Before Computing
%[text] The worked model is a warm object in a room. It starts at 80 degrees Celsius, the room is at 20 degrees Celsius, and the cooling time is 100 s. Predict the direction of the curve and the effect of halving the timestep before reading the output.
%[text] The temperature should decrease toward the room temperature, with a smaller decrease later because the temperature excess becomes smaller. Both Euler runs should begin at 80 degrees Celsius. The 10 s run should be closer to the exact curve than the 20 s run at the same final time.
%%
%[text] ## Model, Assumptions, and Units
%[text] Assume that the object has one uniform temperature, the surroundings remain at a constant temperature, and the cooling time does not change. In words, the cooling rate is the negative temperature excess divided by the cooling time. The state $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius, time $t$ and $\\tau$ are in seconds, and the rate $dT/dt$ is in degrees Celsius per second.
%[text] The model is $dT/dt=-(T-T\_\\mathrm{env})/\\tau$ with $T(0)=T\_0$. Temperature differences in degrees Celsius have the same numerical size as differences in kelvin. The model does not describe internal gradients or changing surroundings.
T_env_C = 20;
T_initial_C = 80;
tau_s = 100;
t_final_s = 200;
dt_choices_s = [20 10];
%%
%[text] ## Algorithm Before MATLAB
%[text] Euler's method holds the present rate fixed for one timestep. The update is $T\_{n+1}=T\_n+\\Delta t\[-(T\_n-T\_\\mathrm{env})/\\tau\]$. Seconds multiplied by degrees Celsius per second give a temperature change, so the update has the unit of temperature.
%[text] - choose the physical parameters and a timestep;
%[text] - make a time array and reserve one temperature value for each time;
%[text] - store the initial condition at the first index;
%[text] - calculate the current rate and use it to write the next index; and
%[text] - compare the computed endpoint with a supplied exact reference. \
%%
%[text] ## Trace the Euler Update at 20 Seconds
%[text] The first code block follows the algorithm directly. MATLAB index 1 stores the value at time zero; each loop pass reads index `n` and writes index `n+1`.
dt_20_s = dt_choices_s(1);
t_20_s = 0:dt_20_s:t_final_s;
T_20_C = zeros(size(t_20_s));
T_20_C(1) = T_initial_C;
for n = 1:numel(t_20_s)-1
    rate_C_per_s = -(T_20_C(n)-T_env_C)/tau_s;
    T_20_C(n+1) = T_20_C(n)+dt_20_s*rate_C_per_s;
end
trace_20 = table(t_20_s(1:3)',T_20_C(1:3)', ... %[output:group:1e4fe812] %[output:37672b1f]
    'VariableNames',{'Time_s','EulerTemperature_C'}) %[output:group:1e4fe812] %[output:37672b1f]
%[text] At 20 s the first Euler value is 68 degrees Celsius. The next update uses the new temperature, not the original 80 degrees Celsius.
%%
%[text] ## Show a Defect Safely
%[text] A supplied or AI-assisted script can run while still encoding the wrong physics. The following isolated calculation uses a plus sign in the rate. It is kept separate from the valid trajectory so the defective result cannot contaminate the evidence.
T_wrong_plus_C = zeros(size(t_20_s));
T_wrong_plus_C(1) = T_initial_C;
for n = 1:numel(t_20_s)-1
    wrong_rate_C_per_s = (T_wrong_plus_C(n)-T_env_C)/tau_s;
    T_wrong_plus_C(n+1) = T_wrong_plus_C(n)+dt_20_s*wrong_rate_C_per_s;
end
wrong_plus_endpoint_C = T_wrong_plus_C(end) %[output:763a69dc]
wrong_plus_sign_is_flagged = wrong_plus_endpoint_C > T_initial_C %[output:6f42134e]
%[text] The plus sign makes a warm object heat away from the room temperature. The defect is visible from the expected direction and from the sign of the rate. The corrected line is `rate_C_per_s = -(T_20_C(n)-T_env_C)/tau_s`, followed by the same timestep multiplication.
%%
%[text] ## Complete the 10-Second Run
%[text] Halve the timestep while keeping every physical parameter and the final time fixed. The shorter step samples the changing rate more often; it does not alter the physical cooling time.
dt_10_s = dt_choices_s(2);
t_10_s = 0:dt_10_s:t_final_s;
T_10_C = zeros(size(t_10_s));
T_10_C(1) = T_initial_C;
for n = 1:numel(t_10_s)-1
    rate_C_per_s = -(T_10_C(n)-T_env_C)/tau_s;
    T_10_C(n+1) = T_10_C(n)+dt_10_s*rate_C_per_s;
end
endpoint_table = table(dt_choices_s',[T_20_C(end);T_10_C(end)], ... %[output:group:5701875e] %[output:3ee0d41d]
    'VariableNames',{'Timestep_s','EulerEndpoint_C'}) %[output:group:5701875e] %[output:3ee0d41d]
%%
%[text] ## Core Validation: Compare With the Exact Reference
%[text] For this model, the supplied exact solution is $T(t)=T\_\\mathrm{env}+(T\_0-T\_\\mathrm{env})\\exp(-t/\\tau)$. It satisfies the same initial condition and cooling law. Evaluate it at the two Euler time arrays so each endpoint comparison uses the same physical time.
T_exact_20_C = T_env_C+(T_initial_C-T_env_C)*exp(-t_20_s/tau_s);
T_exact_10_C = T_env_C+(T_initial_C-T_env_C)*exp(-t_10_s/tau_s);
exact_endpoint_C = T_env_C+(T_initial_C-T_env_C)*exp(-t_final_s/tau_s);
endpoint_error_C = abs([T_20_C(end);T_10_C(end)]-exact_endpoint_C);
reference_table = table(dt_choices_s',[T_20_C(end);T_10_C(end)], ... %[output:group:9e926ef5] %[output:6af37179]
    repmat(exact_endpoint_C,2,1),endpoint_error_C, ... %[output:6af37179]
    'VariableNames',{'Timestep_s','EulerEndpoint_C','ExactEndpoint_C','AbsoluteError_C'}) %[output:group:9e926ef5] %[output:6af37179]
%[text] The exact endpoint is about 28.12 degrees Celsius. The 10 s Euler endpoint should be closer to that reference than the 20 s endpoint. This is the one Core validation check for the worked learning example: compare the reproducible numerical output with a known solution at the same time. The named 1 degree Celsius tolerance is an illustrative teaching threshold for these supplied coarse steps, not a universal accuracy claim.
reference_tolerance_C = 1;
core_reference_check_passed = endpoint_error_C(2) < endpoint_error_C(1) && endpoint_error_C(2) < reference_tolerance_C;
assert(core_reference_check_passed,'The timestep and exact-reference comparison did not pass.')
%%
%[text] ## Lecturer-Led Parameter Modification
%[text] Predict what happens when only the cooling time is changed to 150 s. A larger cooling time means a less negative rate at the same temperature excess, so the object should remain warmer at 200 s. The exact expression gives a complete check of this lecturer-led modification.
tau_changed_s = 150;
changed_tau_exact_endpoint_C = T_env_C+(T_initial_C-T_env_C)*exp(-t_final_s/tau_changed_s);
changed_tau_endpoint_difference_C = changed_tau_exact_endpoint_C-exact_endpoint_C;
changed_tau_check_passed = changed_tau_exact_endpoint_C > exact_endpoint_C;
assert(changed_tau_check_passed,'The slower-cooling parameter prediction failed.')
tau_change_table = table([tau_s;tau_changed_s],[exact_endpoint_C;changed_tau_exact_endpoint_C], ...
    'VariableNames',{'CoolingTime_s','ExactEndpoint_C'})
%[text] The changed endpoint is higher because the same 200 s covers fewer cooling times. This controlled comparison changes one physical parameter and keeps the initial state, room temperature, and final time fixed.
%%
%[text] ## Read the Numerical Evidence
%[text] The native MATLAB graph makes the comparison visible. Markers show computed values; the smooth line is the supplied exact reference. The room-temperature line provides physical orientation and is not a substitute for the endpoint reference check.
t_exact_s = linspace(0,t_final_s,401);
T_exact_fine_C = T_env_C+(T_initial_C-T_env_C)*exp(-t_exact_s/tau_s);
plot(t_exact_s,T_exact_fine_C,'-','Color',[0.25 0.45 0.34],'LineWidth',2) %[output:8aa8067b]
hold on %[output:8aa8067b]
plot(t_20_s,T_20_C,'o-','Color',[0.12 0.28 0.46],'LineWidth',1.5,'MarkerSize',5) %[output:8aa8067b]
plot(t_10_s,T_10_C,'s-','Color',[0.36 0.50 0.66],'LineWidth',1.3,'MarkerSize',4) %[output:8aa8067b]
yline(T_env_C,'--','Room temperature','Color',[0.45 0.45 0.45]) %[output:8aa8067b]
hold off %[output:8aa8067b]
xlabel('Time, t (s)') %[output:8aa8067b]
ylabel('Temperature, T (degrees Celsius)') %[output:8aa8067b]
title('Euler Timestep Audit for Newton Cooling') %[output:8aa8067b]
legend('Exact reference','Euler, dt = 20 s','Euler, dt = 10 s','Location','northeast') %[output:8aa8067b]
grid on %[output:8aa8067b]
%[text] Both Euler curves move in the expected physical direction. The gap to the exact curve is numerical approximation error; a smaller timestep reduces that gap for this run.
%%
%[text] ## Working Exposure: Read a Supplied Solver Reference
%[text] `ode45` is a supplied reference implementation from an earlier week. Read the call as a solver output, not as a new method to derive. The right-hand side is the same cooling model, and the endpoint is compared with the exact expression.
cooling_rate = @(time_s,temp_C) -(temp_C-T_env_C)/tau_s;
[t_ode45_s,T_ode45_C] = ode45(cooling_rate,[0 t_final_s],T_initial_C);
ode45_endpoint_C = T_ode45_C(end);
ode45_reference_difference_C = abs(ode45_endpoint_C-exact_endpoint_C);
ode45_summary = table(ode45_endpoint_C,exact_endpoint_C,ode45_reference_difference_C, ... %[output:group:467084d4] %[output:3e6de375]
    'VariableNames',{'Ode45Endpoint_C','ExactEndpoint_C','AbsoluteDifference_C'}) %[output:group:467084d4] %[output:3e6de375]
%%
%[text] ## Working Exposure: Compare Feasible Evidence Paths
%[text] Several methods can answer related questions, but their evidence has different roles. The table compares a coarse Euler result, a refined Euler result, and the supplied adaptive solver at the same final time. The exact expression remains the reference for this familiar model.
method_comparison = ["Forward Euler, dt = 20 s";"Forward Euler, dt = 10 s";"ode45 supplied reference"];
method_endpoint_C = [T_20_C(end);T_10_C(end);ode45_endpoint_C];
method_error_C = abs(method_endpoint_C-exact_endpoint_C);
method_comparison_table = table(method_comparison,method_endpoint_C,method_error_C, ... %[output:group:3d2f7782] %[output:08844a6d]
    'VariableNames',{'EvidencePath','Endpoint_C','AbsoluteError_C'}) %[output:group:3d2f7782] %[output:08844a6d]
%%
%[text] ## Capstone Evidence Checklist
%[text] A bounded capstone investigation carries its reasoning with its output. The group must state the model, assumptions, variables, and units; complete supplied pseudocode; modify one physical parameter or assumption; show one principal graph or table; perform one required validation check and one chosen check; interpret the result; state one limitation; and record reproducibility and AI decisions. Each member must be able to trace a relevant code section and answer a physics or validation follow-up.
%[text] The required check tests the approved model against a supplied reference, known case, limiting value, residual, or other agreed evidence. The chosen check is a second defensible check selected by the group, such as an initial condition, bound, conservation relation, refinement comparison, or independent calculation. A polished graph without these two checks is incomplete evidence.
%%
%[text] ## Physical Interpretation and Reproducibility
%[text] The model predicts approach toward 20 degrees Celsius. Halving the timestep changes the numerical approximation, while the cooling constant remains 100 s. The exact comparison shows that the 10 s run is more accurate for this case; it does not prove that the uniform-temperature model describes every real object.
%[text] Run this lecture demonstration from top to bottom in a fresh MATLAB R2025a-or-later session using base MATLAB. The locked values are $T\_\\mathrm{env}=20$ degrees Celsius, $T\_0=80$ degrees Celsius, $\\tau=100$ s, final time 200 s, and Euler timesteps 20 s and 10 s. All arrays, parameters, reference values, and checks are created in the script.

